.TH S3AR 1 "August 2026" "S3 Archiver" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
s3ar \- create tar archives from S3 and extract them back to S3
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B s3ar
.RB ( \-c | \-\-create )
.RB [ \-v | \-\-verbose ]
.RB [ \-\-zstd ]
.RB [ \-f " TARFILE" ]
.I S3...
.br
.B s3ar
.RB ( \-x | \-\-extract | \-\-get )
.RB [ \-v | \-\-verbose ]
.RB [ \-\-zstd ]
.RB [ \-f " TARFILE" ]
.RI [ S3... ]
.br
.B s3ar \-\-list-buckets
.RB [ \-v | \-\-verbose ]
.RI [ S3... ]
.br
.B s3ar \-\-list-objects
.RB [ \-v | \-\-verbose ]
.I S3...
.br
.B s3ar
.RB ( \-h | \-\-help )
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B s3ar
(S3 Archiver) is a command-line utility for creating tar archives from an
S3-compatible object store and extracting those archives back to S3. In
addition to object data, the archives store S3 user metadata and bucket ACL
summaries in PAX
.B SCHILY
extended-attribute headers.
.PP
The command-line interface is intentionally designed to resemble the standard
.BR tar (1)
utility. Archive creation and extraction use the familiar
.BR \-c ,
.BR \-x ,
.BR \-f ,
and
.B \-v
options, and selection operands are processed in command-line order.
.B s3ar
is not a full replacement for
.BR tar ;
it applies the same style of operation to live S3 resources and supports only
the options documented here.
.SH BUILDING
On Debian, install the build dependencies and build the executable with:
.PP
.nf
apt install build-essential libs3-dev libarchive-dev
make
.fi
.PP
The resulting executable is
.BR ./s3ar .
.SH S3 CONFIGURATION
Configure S3 access with these environment variables:
.PP
.nf
export S3AR_ACCESS_KEY='access-key'
export S3AR_SECRET_KEY='secret-key'
export S3AR_ENDPOINT='https://s3.example.net'
export S3AR_URI_STYLE='path'
export S3AR_REGION='us-east-1'
.fi
.PP
.BR S3AR_ACCESS_KEY ,
.BR S3AR_SECRET_KEY ,
and
.B S3AR_ENDPOINT
are required.
.B S3AR_ENDPOINT
contains a host and optional port, but no URL path. Its scheme may be
.B http://
or
.BR https:// ;
HTTPS is used when the scheme is omitted. One trailing slash is accepted and
removed.
.PP
.B S3AR_URI_STYLE
accepts
.B path
or
.B virtual
and defaults to
.BR path .
.B S3AR_REGION
selects the S3 region and defaults to
.B us-east-1
when it is not set.
.PP
The bundled libs3 interface does not support temporary session credentials,
so
.B S3AR_SESSION_TOKEN
is not currently supported.
.SS Environment variables
.TP
.B S3AR_ACCESS_KEY
S3 access key. Required.
.TP
.B S3AR_SECRET_KEY
S3 secret key. Required.
.TP
.B S3AR_ENDPOINT
S3 endpoint in the form
.BR [http://|https://]HOST[:PORT] .
Required.
.TP
.B S3AR_URI_STYLE
S3 addressing style. Optional; defaults to
.BR path .
.TP
.B S3AR_REGION
S3 region. Optional; defaults to
.BR us-east-1 .
.SH COMMAND LINE
.SS Operations
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-create
Create a tar archive from one or more selected S3 resources. At least one S3
operand is required. Multiple operands are processed in command-line order.
If overlapping operands select an object more than once, the object is stored
once for each matching operand. Bucket directory members are stored only once.
.TP
.BR \-x ", " \-\-extract ", " \-\-get
Extract a tar archive into S3. Zero or more S3 operands may filter archive
members. Multiple matching filters do not upload an archive member more than
once.
.TP
.B \-\-list-buckets
List all bucket names without listing their objects. Positional arguments are
ignored.
.TP
.B \-\-list-objects
List objects in the selected live S3 resources. At least one S3 operand is
required.
.SS Options
.TP
.BR \-f " " TARFILE ", " \-\-file= TARFILE
Read or write the named archive. This option is valid only with create or
extract. TARFILE must be a local filesystem path or
.BR \- .
Create truncates an existing local file and writes directly to it. If create
fails after opening the file, a partial archive can remain.
.TP
.B \-\-zstd
With create, write a zstd-compressed archive. Extract automatically detects
plain and zstd-compressed archives; specifying this option requires zstd
input. The option is not valid with
.B \-\-list-buckets
or
.BR \-\-list-objects .
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose
Enable verbose output. With create and extract, write each successfully
archived or restored member name as
.B BUCKET/KEY
to standard error, without the
.B s3://
prefix. With listing operations, include the additional fields described in
.BR "LISTING BUCKETS AND OBJECTS" .
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Display command-line help and exit successfully.
.PP
Without
.BR \-f ,
or with
.BR "\-f \-" ,
create writes to standard output while extract reads from standard input.
Extract with no S3 operand restores all accepted archive members.
.SH SELECTING S3 RESOURCES
A trailing slash does not change the selection.
.TP
.B s3://
Select all buckets and all their objects.
.TP
.BR s3://BUCKET " or " s3://BUCKET/
Select the named bucket and all its objects.
.TP
.BR s3://BUCKET/NAME " or " s3://BUCKET/NAME/
Select the exact
.B NAME
key and all objects below
.BR NAME/ .
.PP
Matching occurs only at a path boundary. Selecting
.B photo
can match the exact key
.B photo
and keys such as
.BR photo/first.jpg ,
but never
.B photo1.jpg
or
.BR photo-old.jpg .
The exact key does not need to exist when matching descendants exist.
.PP
An object selection that matches neither an exact key nor any descendant is an
error. A bucket selection succeeds for an empty bucket and still writes the
bucket archive member during creation. Input operands use the
.B s3://
form even though listing and verbose output omit that prefix.
.PP
The following S3 objects are used in the examples below:
.PP
.nf
s3://photos/2026/photo1.jpg
s3://photos/2026/photo2.jpg
s3://photos/2026/photoN.jpg
s3://photos/2027/photo1.jpg
s3://photos/2027/photo2.jpg
s3://photos/2027/photoN.jpg
s3://videos/2026/video1.jpg
s3://videos/2026/video2.jpg
s3://videos/2026/videoN.jpg
s3://videos/2027/video1.jpg
s3://videos/2027/video2.jpg
s3://videos/2027/videoN.jpg
.fi
.SH CREATING ARCHIVES
Create always requires at least one S3 selection operand. To select everything,
pass
.B s3://
explicitly; omitting the operand is an error.
.PP
Back up everything in S3:
.PP
.nf
s3ar \-c \-f media.tar s3://
.fi
.PP
For the example resources above, explicitly selecting both buckets archives
the same objects:
.PP
.nf
s3ar \-c \-f media.tar s3://photos s3://videos
.fi
.PP
Back up exactly one bucket and all its objects:
.PP
.nf
s3ar \-c \-f photos.tar s3://photos
.fi
.PP
Back up only the photos from 2026:
.PP
.nf
s3ar \-c \-f photos-2026.tar s3://photos/2026
.fi
.PP
Back up the photos and videos from 2026:
.PP
.nf
s3ar \-c \-f media-2026.tar s3://photos/2026 s3://videos/2026
.fi
.PP
Without
.BR \-f ,
the archive is streamed to standard output:
.PP
.nf
s3ar \-c s3://photos/2026 >photos-2026.tar
.fi
.PP
Each selected bucket is stored once as a
.B BUCKET/
directory entry. Objects are regular entries named
.BR BUCKET/KEY ;
their bodies are streamed from S3 directly into libarchive. Empty buckets
therefore still produce an archive member.
.PP
The restricted PAX archive stores S3 metadata as SCHILY extended attributes:
.PP
.nf
SCHILY.xattr.s3ar.bucket-acl=public-read,custom
SCHILY.xattr.user.NAME=VALUE
.fi
.PP
Unsafe object keys containing empty,
.BR . ,
or
.B ..
path components are rejected. Create is a weak snapshot: an object can change
between LIST and GET. With
.BR \-v ,
archived member names are written without the
.B s3://
prefix to standard error. This is independent of the archive destination and
keeps a tar stream on standard output unmodified.
.SH EXTRACTING ARCHIVES
Selection operands are optional with extract. With no S3 operand, every
accepted archive member is restored; one or more operands limit extraction to
matching members.
.PP
Assuming
.B media.tar
contains the full backup created above, extract all of it back to S3:
.PP
.nf
s3ar \-x \-f media.tar
.fi
.PP
Extract exactly the
.B photos
bucket and all its objects:
.PP
.nf
s3ar \-x \-f media.tar s3://photos
.fi
.PP
Extract only the photos from 2026:
.PP
.nf
s3ar \-x \-f media.tar s3://photos/2026
.fi
.PP
Multiple operands can select several branches. This extracts the photos and
videos from 2026:
.PP
.nf
s3ar \-x \-f media.tar s3://photos/2026 s3://videos/2026
.fi
.PP
Without
.BR \-f ,
the archive is read from standard input. Selection operands work the same way
as with an archive file:
.PP
.nf
cat media.tar | s3ar \-x s3://photos/2026
.fi
.PP
Bucket directory entries create missing buckets. Object entries are streamed
directly from libarchive into S3 PUT requests and overwrite objects with the
same keys. Missing buckets needed by selected objects are created even when
the archive contains no bucket directory entry.
.PP
Only tar archives containing top-level bucket directories and regular
.B BUCKET/KEY
object members are accepted. Absolute paths, empty,
.BR . ,
and
.B ..
components, links, and other member types are rejected. Uncompressed and
zstd-compressed archives are accepted. A requested operand that matches no
archive member is an error.
.PP
.B SCHILY.xattr.user.NAME
values are restored as S3 user metadata. Bucket ACL summaries are informational
and are not restored; new buckets and uploaded objects use private ACLs. With
.BR \-v ,
restored member names are written without the
.B s3://
prefix to standard error.
.SH LISTING BUCKETS AND OBJECTS
.SS Listing buckets
.B \-\-list-buckets
lists all bucket names without listing their objects:
.PP
.nf
s3ar \-\-list-buckets
.fi
.PP
The names are written to standard output, one per line:
.PP
.nf
photos
videos
.fi
.PP
With
.BR \-v
or
.BR \-\-verbose ,
every name is followed by a tab and its ACL summary:
.PP
.nf
photos	acl=public-read,custom
videos	acl=private
.fi
.PP
An ACL unavailable from the endpoint is shown as
.BR acl=unavailable .
All positional arguments passed to
.B \-\-list-buckets
are ignored. In particular,
.BR s3://asdasd ,
.BR s3://bucket/prefix ,
and
.B s3://
do not filter the output or cause those resources to be accessed.
.SS Listing objects
.B \-\-list-objects
requires one or more S3 selection operands. It writes every selected object to
standard output as
.BR BUCKET/KEY ,
without the
.B s3://
prefix, one object per line. For example:
.PP
.nf
s3ar \-\-list-objects s3://photos/2026
.fi
.PP
produces:
.PP
.nf
photos/2026/photo1.jpg
photos/2026/photo2.jpg
photos/2026/photoN.jpg
.fi
.PP
The operand
.B s3://
lists objects from every bucket. Buckets are processed in sorted order. Object
listings use S3 ordering and continue across pages of up to 512 objects. Bucket
names are not printed separately, so empty buckets produce no output.
.PP
A bucket operand lists all objects in that bucket. An object operand lists the
exact object first, when it exists, followed by matching descendants in S3
order.
.PP
With
.BR \-v
or
.BR \-\-verbose ,
each object is followed by its byte size and sorted S3 user metadata, separated
by tabs:
.PP
.nf
photos/2026/photo1.jpg	1234	sha256=3472a7,source=camera
.fi
.PP
An object without user metadata uses
.BR \- .
Verbose object listings perform one HEAD request per object to retrieve its
user metadata.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The listing or help completed successfully.
.TP
.B 2
Invalid usage, invalid configuration, allocation failure, S3 failure, or
output failure.
.PP
Fatal errors are written to standard error with an
.B s3ar:
prefix.
.SH TESTING WITH THE LOCAL S3 SERVER
Prepare a filesystem-backed test store and start the server:
.PP
.nf
test_data=$(mktemp \-d)
mkdir \-p "$test_data/photos/2026" "$test_data/photos/2027"
mkdir \-p "$test_data/videos/2026" "$test_data/videos/2027"

for year in 2026 2027; do
    for number in 1 2 N; do
        printf 'photo%s from %s\\n' "$number" "$year" \\
            >"$test_data/photos/$year/photo$number.jpg"
        printf 'video%s from %s\\n' "$number" "$year" \\
            >"$test_data/videos/$year/video$number.jpg"
    done
done

python3 ./s3testserver.py "$test_data" \-\-host 127.0.0.1 \-\-port 9000
.fi
.PP
In another shell, configure and run
.BR s3ar :
.PP
.nf
export S3AR_ENDPOINT='http://127.0.0.1:9000'
export S3AR_URI_STYLE='path'
export S3AR_REGION='us-east-1'
export S3AR_ACCESS_KEY='test-access'
export S3AR_SECRET_KEY='test-secret'

\&./s3ar \-\-list-buckets
\&./s3ar \-\-list-objects s3://
\&./s3ar \-\-list-objects s3://photos s3://videos
\&./s3ar \-\-list-objects s3://photos/2026
\&./s3ar \-\-list-objects s3://photos/2026 s3://videos/2026

\&./s3ar \-c \-f media.tar s3://
\&./s3ar \-c \-f media-buckets.tar s3://photos s3://videos
\&./s3ar \-c \-f photos-2026.tar s3://photos/2026
\&./s3ar \-c \-f media-2026.tar s3://photos/2026 s3://videos/2026

\&./s3ar \-x \-f media.tar
\&./s3ar \-x \-f media.tar s3://photos/2026
\&./s3ar \-x \-f media.tar s3://photos/2026 s3://videos/2026
.fi
.PP
The test server imports the filesystem tree at startup. Restart it after
changing files directly in the test-data directory.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR tar (1)
